  "prediction": "**Oldest Cold Case Ever Brought to Court: Man Released After 58 Years**\n\nIn a historic decision, a judge in Illinois has ordered the release of Jack McCullough, 76, who was convicted in 2012 of the 1957 murder of 7-year-old Maria Ridulph. The case, which went unsolved for over five decades, is considered one of the oldest cold cases ever brought to court.\n\nMcCullough, who was serving a life sentence, was granted a new trial and released from prison on Friday after a prosecutor found \"clear and convincing evidence\" that he was wrongly convicted. The decision was made by Judge William Brady, who pointed to newly discovered phone records that support McCullough's long-held alibi that he was in Rockford, Illinois, at the time of the murder, rather than in Sycamore, where the crime took place.\n\nThe case against McCullough was built on testimony from Maria's friend, Kathy Sigman, who identified him as the killer in 2011, five decades after the crime. However, prosecutors later found that the identification was based on a flawed lineup and that McCullough's alibi had been improperly discredited.\n\nDeKalb County State's Attorney Richard Schmack, who reviewed the case and concluded that McCullough was innocent, said that the evidence against him was \"absolutely nothing\" and that the documents contained \"a wealth of information pointing to McCullough's innocence.\"\n\nMaria's family, who had been convinced of McCullough's guilt, are now reeling from the decision. Maria's brother, Charles Ridulph, was in attendance at the hearing and displayed little emotion as the judge's decision was announced.\n\nThe case has raised questions about the reliability of eyewitness testimony and the importance of properly investigating alibis. It also highlights the challenges of prosecuting cold cases, where evidence is often lost or destroyed and memories fade over time.\n\nMcCullough's release is a significant development in a case that has captivated the nation 